WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.880
SAST fits across the secure software development lifecycle, from development through maintenance.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.700
It can support requirements and design by showing recurring implementation risks that should be addressed earlier.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.560
It can support coding by giving developers feedback near the time a change is written.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:26.860
It can support code review, build, test, release, and maintenance by helping teams identify new issues, track existing risk, and verify that fixes remain in place.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.840
The strongest SAST programs shift feedback earlier without turning every finding into a late-stage crisis.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.980
Developer desktop checks can catch common issues before a pull request.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.880
Pull request scanning can focus reviewers on new risky patterns.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.780
CI/CD pipeline checks can provide consistent enforcement.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.120
Release gates can highlight unresolved high-risk issues.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.180
Periodic full scans can uncover older or inherited problems that did not appear in a single change.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.520
Legacy application onboarding deserves a thoughtful approach.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:06.300
A first scan of a mature codebase may produce many findings, including old issues, duplicates, noisy rules, and patterns that need context.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:13.240
A baseline scan can establish known existing findings so the team can track new issues and plan remediation over time.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.380
Without a baseline strategy, SAST can feel like a wall of debt instead of a practical improvement program.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.440
SAST should support developers, not become a noisy final gate.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:31.340
It complements threat modeling, manual review, dependency management, runtime testing, monitoring, and vulnerability response.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.800
Threat modeling helps define what matters.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.260
Manual review adds context.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.720
Dependency scanning sees component risk.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.100
Runtime testing observes behavior.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:48.520
SAST contributes source-level signal, and that signal is most useful when it arrives in time to guide engineering decisions.
